WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.250
The end of World War II also marked the beginning
of the nuclear weapons race and, as a direct

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:09.180
consequence, the inauguration of the first
atomic weapons tests.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.301
According to data provided by the Stockholm
International Peace Research Institute, as

00:00:13.301 --> 00:00:19.270
many as two thousand fifty-three nuclear tests
were conducted in the period from 1945 to

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:24.420
2006 in all global environments, that is,
in the atmosphere and sometimes underwater

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:25.860
(25 percent) and underground (75 percent).

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:30.880
Just to mention a couple of them: in 1954
the United States tested the potential of

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.420
the hydrogen bomb in the Marshall Islands,
Bikini Atoll, and 7 years later the Soviet

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:40.520
Union tested the Tsar bomb, or what is believed
to be the most powerful hydrogen bomb ever

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:44.730
conceived, in the Novaja Zemlja Archipelago,
north of the Ural Mountains.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:49.690
The severe environmental damage caused by
these tests laid the groundwork for the drafting

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:55.559
in 1963 of the Partial Test Ban Treaty, a
very first treaty to ban experiments conducted

00:00:55.559 --> 00:00:57.920
in all environments except underground.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:03.239
Even if the treaty wasn’t ratified by France
and China, it placed a first major limit on

00:01:03.239 --> 00:01:07.689
the spread of radioactive isotopes in the
atmosphere, and if we combine its normative

00:01:07.689 --> 00:01:13.630
precepts with those contained in the 1968
Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty and the subsequent

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:19.000
1996 Comprehensive Nuclear Test Ban Treaty,
at first glance we would think that the path

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.250
taken was the right one.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:24.759
In fact, we are well aware that today there
are nuclear-weapon countries that aren’t

00:01:24.759 --> 00:01:27.030
party to any nonproliferation treaty.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:31.880
Think of India, Pakistan, North Korea and
apparently Israel (but this isn’t confirmed),

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:34.579
and they continue to conduct nuclear tests.

00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:39.710
But on balance, the countries most responsible
for the global environmental spread of radionuclides

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:44.159
remain the United States and Russia, with
a good share of the blame also falling on

00:01:44.159 --> 00:01:46.439
China, France and the United Kingdom.

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:50.920
As the Comprehensive Nuclear Test Ban Treaty
Organization (CTBTO) reports, the former are

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.590
blamed for the largest number of experiments.

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:58.880
If we exclude the 66 conducted in the North
Pacific, of which we recall the most notable,

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:04.229
that of Castle Bravo in the Bikini Atoll,
most of them involved North American soil,

00:02:04.229 --> 00:02:09.330
particularly the Nevada desert, a region that
records 44 percent of all global tests.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:14.550
By contrast, a slightly lower percentage belongs
to the former Soviet Union, responsible for

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.270
35 percent of the world's nuclear tests.

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:22.550
In addition to the already mentioned Novaja
Zemlja, the Semipalatinsk region of Kazakhstan

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:27.300
has been affected, in particular the Ground
Zero site in the north center and Lake Balapan

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:33.270
in the south, whose waterways now have concentrations
of radioactive uranium isotopes far in excess

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:36.310
of those permitted by the World Health Organization.

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:42.400
China, with its 23 tests conducted during
the period from 1964 to 1980 in the Tarim

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:47.590
Basin in Lop Nur, not only further contaminated
the Semipalatinsk region but also most of

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:53.481
Xinjiang province, where today the incidence
of cancer is estimated to be 30-35 percent

00:02:53.481 --> 00:02:55.680
higher than the national average rate.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.720
What if we point the finger at India and Pakistan?

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.470
Well we remain quite disappointed, so to speak,
because it appears that there were no significant

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:08.320
cases of environmental contamination, due
to the very low number of tests conducted.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:13.210
Instead, the United Kingdom was responsible
for the pollution of large areas of Australian

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:20.530
territory, as a result of 12 atmospheric tests
conducted between 1952 and 1956 at Maralinga,

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:22.090
Emu Field and Montebello island.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:29.140
And the french long arm was found first in
Algeria between 1960 and 1966, at Reggane

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:36.500
and In Eker, and then from 1966 to 1996 in
Polynesia, more specifically Mururoa and Fangataufa

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:41.700
atolls in the southeastern part of the Tuamotu-Gambier
archipelago, resulting in contamination of

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:43.870
the marine environment and the food chain.

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:48.960
This is why atmospheric testing, along with
underwater testing, was later replaced by

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:50.480
underground testing.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:55.700
As we said, the French nuclear tests in Polynesia,
a total of one hundred seventy-nine carried

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:57.154
out in Mururoa Atoll (forty-two atmospheric
and one hundred thirty-seven underground)

00:03:57.154 --> 00:03:59.880
and fourteen in Fangataufa Atoll (four atmospheric
and ten underground), haven’t only caused

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:04.380
damage to the surrounding marine ecosystem
and the morphology of the atolls, resulting

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:09.180
in the risk of collapse and loss of height
above sea level, but have also caused devastating

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:14.540
effects on the local populations due to nuclear
waste contaminating food and water.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:20.060
We mentioned that until 1974 French tests
in the Pacific were atmospheric, meaning that

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:24.620
the devices were either detonated at ground
level on the earth, attached to the top of

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:29.680
a tower, or dropped from a plane or carried
in the air by a balloon and detonated hundreds

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:31.470
of meters above sea level.

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:36.000
This was done despite the establishment of
the aforementioned Partial Test Ban Treaty

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:42.520
of 1963 that banned various types of experiments,
including atmospheric ones, which was ratified

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:47.380
by the United Kingdom, the United States and
the Soviet Union but, as it happens, not by

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:48.380
France.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:53.790
But what is the peculiarity that characterizes
these tests as opposed to underground tests?

00:04:53.790 --> 00:05:00.060
Well simple, their greater likelihood of dispersing
radioactive material over large areas.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:04.330
Specifically in Polynesia, the scale of the
experiments ended up affecting not only its

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:09.630
most populated island, Tahiti, but even, according
to a study by the International Physicians

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:13.580
for the Prevention of Nuclear War (IPPNW),
the entire South Pacific region.

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:18.530
To test its actual impact, New Zealand has
installed a series of survey stations since

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:19.730
1957.

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:24.780
Both in countries linked to it as their colonial
authority, (see the Cook Islands, Niue, Tokelau

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:30.000
and Samoa), and in countries that were part
of the United Kingdom's colonial administration

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.081
and so directly dependent on it (Fiji, Tonga
and the former colonies of the Gilbert and

00:05:35.081 --> 00:05:39.910
Ellice Islands - now independent under the
respective names of the Republic of Kiribati

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:40.950
and Tuvalu).

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:46.260
According to the analysis of samples taken
from air, water, milk and fish, nuclear fallout,

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:50.720
that is, the fallout of radioactive particles
on the earth's surface as a result of the

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:55.900
various tests, would have affected at least
6 million people in the countries just mentioned.

00:05:55.900 --> 00:06:00.840
What remains to be understood, however, is
the lethal dosage to which these people were

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:04.540
subjected, which to date is still unclear
and difficult to determine.

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:09.830
In any case, Fiji, Samoa, Tonga, and Tuvalu,
once they gained independence and became member

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:15.080
states of the United Nations, strongly opposed
the implementation of further experiments.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:21.250
Samoa, which became independent in 1962, forged
ties with New Zealand in this regard, ratifying

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:25.840
a treaty of friendship by which they would
pledge mutual assistance in protecting common

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:27.240
diplomatic interests.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:31.830
The same is true for the Cook Islands, Niue
and Tokelau, which are also strongly and directly

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:34.990
linked to New Zealand in several ways, including
defense.

00:06:34.990 --> 00:06:39.480
The longstanding ties between New Zealand
and these island countries have led to the

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:44.940
emergence of several official statements of
protest and several articles openly condemning

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:45.990
the French tests.

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:52.000
Recall, for example, the New Zealand government's
decision dating back to July 1973 to send

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.590
the RNZN (Royal Navy New Zealand) frigate
Otago to Mururoa with Cabinet Minister Fraser

00:06:56.590 --> 00:07:01.520
Coleman on board, to promote protest against
the series of atmospheric tests that were

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:03.370
to be held shortly thereafter.

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:08.630
Then in 1985, most Pacific states gathered
in Rarotonga, Cook Islands, to ratify the

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:11.230
South Pacific Nuclear Free Zone (SPNFZ) Treaty.

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:16.199
Its preamble expresses the desire "to ensure
[...] that the bounty and beauty of the land

00:07:16.199 --> 00:07:20.760
and sea in their region remain the heritage
of their peoples and their descendants in

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:25.900
perpetuity to be enjoyed by all in peace"
and "to keep the region free from environmental

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:30.990
pollution by radioactive wastes and other
radioactive matter" In more recent times,

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:35.130
world public opinion has brought the issue
back into the spotlight.

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:39.360
Indeed, thanks to a joint study conducted
by Princeton University, the French news site

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:44.130
Disclose and the British company Interprt,
the true impact of the tests in the region

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:45.130
has been defined.

00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:50.140
The analysis, made possible by declassified
French military documents regarding the explosion

00:07:50.140 --> 00:07:54.729
of the nuclear devices, estimates that some
one hundred and ten thousand people, including

00:07:54.729 --> 00:07:59.650
eighty thousand residents of Papeete, Tahiti's
main city, were affected by the radioactive

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:00.740
fallout.

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:05.400
According to the investigation, the resulting
radiation would have been 2 to 10 times higher

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:10.710
than the estimates provided by the French
Atomic Energy Commission (CEA) in a 2006 report.

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:14.400
One reason for the discrepancy would be that
the Commission "didn’t always take into

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:19.830
account the drinking of contaminated rainwater"
when calculating the dose of radiation individuals

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:22.160
were likely to have been exposed to.

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:27.270
Reason being, in 2014 the French Polynesia
Assembly sought nearly $1 billion ($930 mln)

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:29.620
in compensation for the damage suffered.

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:36.050
In 2016 Fiji, Nauru, Palau, Samoa, and Tuvalu
submitted an official document advancing multilateral

00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:41.059
nuclear disarmament negotiations in Geneva
and highlighting that condemnation initiatives

00:08:41.059 --> 00:08:46.019
were the only possible approach by Pacific
island states having suffered nuclear tests

00:08:46.019 --> 00:08:47.639
in the region for half a century.

00:08:47.639 --> 00:08:52.589
It added, here I quote, "Their impact on our
fragile ecology and the physical health and

00:08:52.589 --> 00:08:55.110
mental wellbeing of our people has been profound.

00:08:55.110 --> 00:09:00.589
We continue to experience epidemics of cancer,
chronic disease and congenital abnormalities

00:09:00.589 --> 00:09:06.069
as a result of the radioactive fallout that
blanketed our homes and the vast Pacific Ocean

00:09:06.069 --> 00:09:08.389
on which we depend for our livelihood.

00:09:08.389 --> 00:09:11.660
[...] [Pacific residents] have suffered, and
continue to suffer."

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:16.720
Moving elsewhere, however, France conducted
not only nuclear but also chemical and bacteriological

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:19.240
weapons tests on Algerian soil.

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:24.170
And to this day, radioactive remnants of France's
atomic infrastructure remain buried in the

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:26.959
sand or circulate freely on Saharan soil.

00:09:26.959 --> 00:09:34.199
There were as many as 17 nuclear tests in
the Sahara Desert between 1960 and 1966: 4

00:09:34.199 --> 00:09:37.290
atmospheric between 1960 and 1961 at the Centre
Saharien D'Expérimentations militaires (CSEM)

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:41.680
at Reggane on the Tanezrouft plain, about
one thousand five hundred kilometers south

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:42.999
of Algiers.

00:09:42.999 --> 00:09:47.459
To detonate the 4 bombs, the army defined
an immense area of about one hundred thousand

00:09:47.459 --> 00:09:52.480
square kilometers that included a number of
functional zones connected by paved roads:

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:58.320
an existing and inhabited Saharan town, Reggane-Ville,
located near an oasis; a base camp called

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:03.970
Reggane-Plateau for about ten thousand civilian
and military personnel, with labs for employees

00:10:03.970 --> 00:10:09.260
of the Atomic Energy Commission; an advanced
base, Hamoudia; and a ground zero area where

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:11.160
the bombs would be detonated.

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:15.499
The first of the 4, codenamed Blue Jerboa
(Gerboise bleue) and with an explosive yield

00:10:15.499 --> 00:10:21.370
of about 60-70 kilotons (about 4 times that
of Little Boy, the bomb dropped on Hiroshima

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:25.610
in 1945) was detonated on February 13, 1960.

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:29.839
Starting the following year, the tests, at
the behest of the Algerian authorities, had

00:10:29.839 --> 00:10:35.270
to be conducted underground, so they were
moved about 700 km southeast, locality In

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:36.910
Eker, in the Hoggar Mountains.

00:10:36.910 --> 00:10:42.559
The next 13 bombs, codenamed after gemstones,
were detonated in so-called firing tunnels,

00:10:42.559 --> 00:10:47.240
dug horizontally into the rock, using a technique
inspired by U.S. experiments in Nevada.

00:10:47.240 --> 00:10:52.040
But 4 of them, Beryl, Améthyste, Rubis and
Jade, were not fully contained.

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:57.999
In particular, the Beryl bomb, detonated on
May 1, 1962, 2 months before the celebration

00:10:57.999 --> 00:11:02.769
of Algeria's independence, caused the most
dangerous accident of the 4, exposing local

00:11:02.769 --> 00:11:07.410
peoples and civilian and military personnel
to lethal levels of radiation.

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:11.660
To date, these affairs remain classified,
but there are those who have stepped forward

00:11:11.660 --> 00:11:13.100
to shed light on the issue.

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:18.610
Last February, Algerian lawyer and human rights
activist Fatma Zohra Benbraham, in a complaint

00:11:18.610 --> 00:11:23.269
filed before the International Criminal Court
in The Hague, pointed to France's categorical

00:11:23.269 --> 00:11:28.910
refusal to acknowledge that its experiments
had impacted an area at least 700 kilometers

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:29.910
in diameter.

00:11:29.910 --> 00:11:34.939
It goes on to add that as a result of the
same, especially the Reggane site, registers

00:11:34.939 --> 00:11:39.569
several cases of cancers, hypertension, malformations
and other diseases.

00:11:39.569 --> 00:11:44.939
Even if France, but not only it, is required
"to fulfill its responsibilities to ensure

00:11:44.939 --> 00:11:49.749
that sites [...] are monitored scrupulously
and to take appropriate steps to avoid adverse

00:11:49.749 --> 00:11:54.379
impacts on health, safety and the environment
as a consequence of such nuclear testing"

00:11:54.379 --> 00:11:57.529
(IAEA General Conference, Resolution 1995),
no decontamination has yet been initiated.

00:11:57.529 --> 00:12:03.050
In any case, today neither the Algerian government
nor the international community has yet pressed

00:12:03.050 --> 00:12:08.199
the French government for clarity on the issue
or pressed for greater transparency on such

00:12:08.199 --> 00:12:11.670
a painful page in the history of Franco-Algerian
relations.

00:12:11.670 --> 00:12:13.459
Well, we are done for today.

00:12:13.459 --> 00:12:16.989
I thank you all for your attention and see
you in the next video.

00:12:16.989 --> 00:12:17.359
Ciao!

